course: dictionary
lesson: 6938
title: A pail of clues
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A pail is a bucket, a round container with a handle for carrying water, milk or sand. «Jack and Jill went up the hill to fetch a pail of water.» The words around pail tell you what it is for: you fetch water in it.
explain: Context can also show you what a pail holds. In «a pail of whitewash», the pail carries paint, not water. The Mole was spring-cleaning, so whitewash is paint for walls, and a pail is the thing you carry it in.
explain: Pail is mostly used in the US, in old stories and nursery rhymes. In maths problems, pails hold amounts of water or milk that you add, share or measure. When you are unsure, swap in the meaning "bucket" and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: A pail is a bucket with a handle.
fact: «A pail of water» means a bucket filled with water.
fact: «A milk pail» is a pail used for milk.
fact: In maths problems, pails hold amounts you can add, share or measure.
fact: Pail is mostly used in the US and appears in old stories and nursery rhymes.
exercise: «Jack and Jill went up the hill to fetch a pail of water.» What is a pail? | A small cup for tea | A steep path up a hill | *A bucket with a handle for carrying water
exercise: The Mole carried a pail of whitewash. Which of these could a pail hold? | A tall tree | A bicycle | Milk | *Water
exercise: «A pail of whitewash» contains a word that means white paint for walls. Find and copy that word. | *whitewash | pail | dusters | brooms
test: Which phrase means a bucket used for milk? | a pail of sand, which is for the beach | a milk jug with a spout | *a milk pail
test: Where would you most often meet the word pail? | In a shop selling bicycle parts | In a science report about the weather on Mars | *In old stories and nursery rhymes
exam: In «a pail of whitewash», what does pail mean? | A hole dug in the ground | A duster made of soft cloth | A broom for sweeping floors | *A bucket that holds the white paint
